module Darklang.Cli.Docs.Traits

let content () : String =
  """# Traits

A trait names one or more fns over one open type. An implementation says how one
type does them. A call picks the implementation by the type of the value it is
given. The stdlib traits are the operators (`+` is `Add`, `==` is `Eq`) and
`ToString`, so every builtin type already has implementations; yours join with
one `impl`.

## Implement a trait that exists
  type Point = { x: Int64; y: Int64 }

  impl ToString for Point =
    let toString (p: Point) : String =
      $"({Stdlib.Int64.toString p.x}, {Stdlib.Int64.toString p.y})"

  impl Add for Point =
    let add (a: Point) (b: Point) : Point =
      Point { x = a.x + b.x; y = a.y + b.y }

  let p = Point { x = 1L; y = 2L }
  ToString.toString p          # "(1, 2)"
  p.toString                   # the same call, receiver first
  p + p                        # Point { x = 2L; y = 4L }

  A member can name a fn that already exists instead of declaring one:
    impl ToString for Int64 =
      let toString = Stdlib.Int64.toString

## Declare your own
  trait Area<'a> =
    let area (shape: 'a) : Float

  impl Area for Circle =
    let area (c: Circle) : Float = 3.14159 * c.r * c.r

  impl<'a: Area> Area for List<'a> =     # conditional: needs Area for the element
    let area (xs: List<'a>) : Float =
      Stdlib.sum 0.0 (Stdlib.List.map xs (fun s -> Area.area s))

  The first type parameter is the type an implementation is for. A method may
  carry an effect row (`let fetch (u: 'a) :{Http} String`); every
  implementation must fit under it.

## Bound a type parameter
  let describe<'a: ToString> (v: 'a) : String = "value: " ++ v.toString
  let biggest<'a: Ord + ToString> (xs: List<'a>) : String = ...

  `'a: ToString` means "any type with a ToString implementation". It is checked
  when the fn is entered, against the caller's actual type, and statically by
  the checker (`MissingImpl`, `AmbiguousImpl`, `UnboundTypeParameter`).

## Where they live
  A trait is a package item like a type or a fn: `Stdlib.ToString`. An
  implementation is one too, named for the type and the trait under the
  module it was written in: `impl ToString for Point` inside `Acme` is
  `Acme.Point.ToString`, and its method fn is `Acme.Point.ToString.toString`.

    dark ls Acme               # Traits and Implementations sections
    dark view Stdlib.ToString  # prints the trait back
    dark view Acme.Point.ToString
    dark impls ToString        # every implementation, as dispatch sees them
    dark search Area --trait   # --impl for implementations

  Author from the shell like `fn` and `type`; an impl's path is the module it
  is written in:
    dark trait Acme.Area "<'a> = let area (shape: 'a) : Float"
    dark impl Acme "Area for Circle = let area (c: Circle) : Float = ..."

  A bare trait name (`Area`, `Add`) resolves from your module outward, then as
  `Stdlib.<name>`; write the full path when two traits share a name.

## The stdlib traits
  Add Sub Mul Div Mod Pow Neg    the operators `+ - * / % **` and `-x`
  Ord                            `< <= > >=`; also `Stdlib.max`, `min`, `clamp`
  Eq                             `==` and `!=`; structural unless implemented
  ToString                       `x.toString`, `Stdlib.toString x`
  Zero, One                      `Zero.zero<Int64> ()`; `Stdlib.sum seed xs`,
                                 `product seed xs` take a seed so `[]` never asks

  Every builtin numeric type implements the operators; String implements Add
  (`"a" + "b"`) and ToString. `==` compares every value structurally unless its
  type implements `Eq`; builtin types are never overridden, and `List.sort`,
  dict keys and the walk inside a container stay structural. `docs operators`
  has the operator table.

## Two implementations for one type
  An implementation is visible where its name is bound (main, or your branch).
  Two visible ones for one trait and type is an ambiguity: the call says so,
  `dark constraints` keeps it as a finding, and deprecating one resolves it
  (a deprecated implementation is not dispatched to). Two traits with the same
  methods and shapes are one trait, whatever they are named.

## Errors
  No implementation of Stdlib.Add for Bool    # write `impl Add for Bool = ...`
  More than one implementation of ...         # `dark impls <Trait>` lists them
  Cannot tell which type to dispatch Zero.zero on   # no argument carries the
                                              # type: `Zero.zero<Int64> ()`
  Cannot perform numeric operation on Int64 and Float   # operands must match"""
